th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/integration-by-substitution-type-1.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/integration-by-substitution-type-1.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the starting point of integration by substitution Type 1?","options":["Propose a new variable of the form $t = g(x)$.","Propose a parametrisation of the form $x = \\varphi(t)$.","Differentiate both sides of an equation twice.","Replace every function by its inverse function."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab66f348541a93cf2f9badaa","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $t = x^3+2x+3$, what is $\\mathrm{d}t$?","options":["$(x^2+2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$(3x+2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$(x^3+2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$(3x^2+2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b065029d07383c7e48f58aa9","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_007","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A student writes $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\overset{t=\\cos x}{=}\\int_1^1(\\cdots)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=0$. What is wrong?","options":["Substitution needs $g=\\cos$ to be invertible, and $\\cos$ is not invertible on $[0,2\\pi]$.","The limits are wrong: $t$ should run from $1$ to $-1$, not from $1$ to $1$.","Nothing is wrong: the integral is $0$ by the symmetry of $\\sin$ on $[0,2\\pi]$.","$\\sin x$ is not a function of $\\cos x$ on $[0,2\\pi]$, so $\\sin^2x$ is not $f(\\cos x)(\\cos x)'$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5e394d2758a1f838c8423b2","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ be continuous on $[-1,1]$. What is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi} f(\\sin x)\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$0$","$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$2\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","Substitution gives no answer, since $\\sin$ is not injective on $[0,\\pi]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4205b61f552594f54055e62","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_012_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Using $t = x^3+2x+3$, which transformed integral equals $\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 \\frac{3x^2+2}{x^3+2x+3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_6^{15} \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_6^{15} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"efcae76446c13011a7dc6cd6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_014_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"The substitution $t=x^2$ turns which integral directly into $\\int\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\int 2x\\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int \\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int 2\\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int x^2\\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c674d3be2b151c1b79706daa","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $g(x)=x^2-1$ for $x\\in[0,2]$ and $f(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ for $t\\neq0$. A student applies Type 1 substitution with $t=g(x)$ to $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\frac{2x}{x^2-1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ and writes $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{3}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Which assessment of this step is correct?","options":["It is valid, and the value is $\\ln|3|-\\ln|-1|=\\ln 3$.","It is invalid, because $g'(x)=2x$ equals $0$ at $x=0$.","It is invalid, because $f$ is not continuous on the range $[-1,3]$ of $g$.","It is invalid, because the integrand is not of the form $f(g(x))g'(x)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a1fe1fbbbb17c6a2bb327e1","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression gives all antiderivatives $\\displaystyle\\int x\\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, where $C$ is an arbitrary constant?","options":["$\\sin(x^2)+C$","$\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(x^2)+C$","$2\\sin(x^2)+C$","None by Type 1, since the factor $2x$ is missing"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d8449bc77d83e6381852bc95","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_topup20261028_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Using the substitution $t=\\cos x$, which integral equals $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2x\\,\\sin x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1}(-t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^{0}t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}(-t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^{0}(-t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ae1ec2c66891078418a9e78d","time_limit":30}]}}